<h2>How anonymisation works in practice</h2>
<p>In Delhi's district courts and the High Court, the protection translates into working conventions: cause titles carry initials or descriptions rather than names; certified copies and judgments mask the identity particulars; victim details travel in sealed covers where needed; and in-camera trial under POCSO keeps the public out of the courtroom altogether. Counsel on both sides share the obligation — pleadings, bail applications, appeals and even office files must carry the anonymisation through, because a disclosure in a publicly accessible filing is as much a breach as a news report. Orders uploaded to court websites are expected to be redaction-checked before publication.</p>

<h2>The digital dimension</h2>
<p>The provisions were drafted for print and broadcast, but their heaviest contemporary work is online. Social-media posts naming a child, forwarded videos of an incident, screenshots of an FIR, and local-news pages that identify the school or family all fall squarely within the prohibitions — and each share is a fresh disclosure by the person sharing. Takedown is available through the platform's legal process and through directions in the criminal proceedings; prosecution of the originator proceeds under Section 23(4) POCSO and Section 72 BNS. Families should preserve the offending URLs and report quickly: identification spreads in hours, and the practical remedy is speed.</p>

<h2>Why the rule is absolute in design</h2>
<p>The statutory scheme reflects a legislative judgment that identification compounds the harm of the offence itself — in stigma, in the child's rehabilitation, and in the willingness of families to report at all. That is why the exception is child-centric rather than publisher-centric: the only question the Special Court may ask is whether disclosure serves the child, never whether the story is important. It is also why the net is cast over indirect identification; in a neighbourhood or school community, a handful of particulars identifies a child as effectively as a name.</p>
<p>For practitioners, the discipline is simple to state and demanding to keep: anonymise at the first document and audit every subsequent one. For families, the rule is protection, not silence — reporting the offence, pursuing the trial and seeking compensation all proceed fully with the identity sealed.</p>

